OCGRAPH 1
# A THING THAT SCORES. Same reaction shape as test-content/AN_Playable's own Target.ocgraph -- ENTRY
# OnHit, a VAR that survives the tick, a SEPARATE GetVar for reporting than the one read before the
# write (see that file's own comment for the off-by-one this avoids). Two things are new here:
#
# 1. THE VAR IS CALLED `score`, NOT `hits`, and that naming is the answer to this template's "a score
#    that persists across ticks and is logged" requirement. There is deliberately no SEPARATE combined
#    total anywhere (not on the game mode, not anywhere else): FireEvent carries NO PAYLOAD -- the
#    receiving ENTRY gets only entity/time/deltaTime, nothing an amount or a source could ride on
#    (docs/AVER_NODE_NODES.md's Actor section says so plainly) -- and AN_FPRules.ocgraph (the game
#    mode) has no way to learn a target's entity id in the first place: it is PLACED by the map, not
#    spawned by anything, so spawn-and-remember (this template's only id-discovery mechanism -- see
#    AN_FPCharacter.ocgraph) never runs for it. A single combined score would need one of those two
#    gaps closed; this template does not pretend otherwise. Each target's own `score` VAR IS the
#    score, per instance, exactly as honest as the mechanism actually is.
# 2. OnStart SCALES ITSELF UP. The built-in sphere (Meshes/sphere.ocmesh) is generated at radius 1cm
#    (GameContent.cpp's own comment: "the unit cube stays half-extent 1", and the sphere is built the
#    same way) -- a 1cm dot is not a target anyone could see, let alone walk up to. `Spawn` has no
#    scale input at all (only x/y/z), so there is no placement-time way to size a spawned instance;
#    OnStart doing it with a SetFieldVec3 write is the only lever this vocabulary has.
# NAME is a single token (see AN_FPCharacter.ocgraph's own comment on this record -- it truncates
# silently, not an error, so it is worth getting right rather than discovering it in a log line).
NAME FPTarget
DESCRIPTION Scales itself up, counts the hits it receives as its own score. Independent per instance.

CLASS AN_FPTarget Actor mesh=Meshes/sphere.ocmesh material=M_Target

PARAM entity int

NODE ent Param param=entity

VAR score float 0

# ---- self-sizing --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SetFieldVec3 has NO EXEC PINS by default -- it is a plain data write, safe to repeat, dispatched
# "SetField-style" (see docs/AVER_NODE_NODES.md's Scene section). That is exactly right for a
# no-ENTRY dataflow graph, and exactly wrong here: THIS graph has three ENTRY records (seed, hit,
# tick), so it compiles PUSH, and a PUSH graph refuses to pull a side-effecting node with no incoming
# exec edge. Reaching it from OnStart means overriding every one of its seven pins by hand -- the
# identical trap, and the identical fix, test-content/GraphDemo's IdleMotion.ocgraph already paid for
# on its own `place` node (docs/AVER_NODE_NODES.md trap #1 is that exact example). Miss even one of
# the seven PIN lines below and it does not fall back to a default -- it is just gone.
ENTRY seed OnStart
NODE seed OnStart

NODE sx ConstFloat
PIN sx value out float 60.0
NODE sy ConstFloat
PIN sy value out float 60.0
NODE sz ConstFloat
PIN sz value out float 60.0

NODE grow SetFieldVec3 field=CLocal.scale
PIN grow exec in exec
PIN grow entity in int
PIN grow x in float
PIN grow y in float
PIN grow z in float
PIN grow then out exec
PIN grow success out bool
LINK seed.exec grow.exec
LINK ent.value grow.entity
LINK sx.value grow.x
LINK sy.value grow.y
LINK sz.value grow.z

# ---- the reaction -- identical shape to Target.ocgraph's own hit chain -----------------------------
ENTRY hit OnHit
NODE hit OnHit

NODE one ConstFloat
PIN one value out float 1.0

NODE prev GetVar var=score
NODE next Add
LINK prev.value next.a
LINK one.value next.b

NODE store SetVar var=score
LINK hit.exec store.exec
LINK next.result store.value

# ---- reporting -- a SEPARATE GetVar from `prev`, for the same reason Target.ocgraph's own comment
# gives: pulling `prev` here would report the score as it stood BEFORE this tick's hit (if any) was
# stored -- an off-by-one that reads as a lost point. Reading back through its own node, after the
# write, is the honest shape.
ENTRY tick OnTick
NODE tick OnTick
NODE report GetVar var=score

OUT report value
